<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Turdetani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The heirs of Tartessos in the lower Guadalquivir, praised by Strabo as the most cultured of the Iberians, with written laws in verse.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Turdetani appear after the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tartessos">Tartessos</a> around 500 BCE, occupying the same rich lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> and Cádiz. Ancient writers saw them as the successors of the Tartessians.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in many towns ruled by local kings and aristocracies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called them the wisest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>, said to have laws in verse and an ancient literature. Their land produced grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and wine and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-tinto">Río Tinto</a>. They were heavily influenced by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gadir">Gadir</a> and the other Phoenician cities, and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a> they supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and troops. After 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took control; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culchas">Culchas</a>, who ruled 28 towns, first aided Rome and then joined the revolt of 197 BCE. Defeated, they became the core of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Baetica</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turdetani.jpg" alt="Turdetani" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: Alcides Pinto; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iberia_300BC-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 206 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Culchas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turdetanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turdetanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-353&amp;focus=turdetani" aria-label="Turdetani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4566 751 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turdetani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4845 1101h0M4975 1081h0M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4886 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4886 1007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=-353&amp;focus=turdetani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
